course: geography
lesson: 8
title: The British Isles and the United Kingdom
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Three names get mixed up all the time: Great Britain is an island, the United Kingdom is a country, and the British Isles is a name for a whole group of islands.
explain: Great Britain holds England, Scotland and Wales; the UK adds Northern Ireland; and the Republic of Ireland is a separate country that shares the island of Ireland.
explain: Start EU5 in 1337 and England and Scotland are rival kingdoms, while England's grip on Ireland barely reaches beyond Dublin; the kingdom of Great Britain comes only in 1707.
explain: Football tells the story: England, Scotland, Wales and Northern Ireland play as four teams, because the UK is made of four nations.
explain: The islands are small but never far from the sea: nowhere in the UK is more than about 113 km from tidal water, which helped make Britain a sea-trading power.
fact: The British Isles is a geographical name for Great Britain, Ireland and over 6,000 smaller islands, but many people in Ireland dislike the name and the Irish government does not use it.
fact: Great Britain is the island holding England, Scotland and Wales, while the United Kingdom of Great Britain and Northern Ireland also includes Northern Ireland.
fact: The UK is made of four countries, or nations: England (capital London), Scotland (Edinburgh), Wales (Cardiff) and Northern Ireland (Belfast).
fact: Wales was joined to England by laws of 1535 and 1542, England and Scotland shared a king from 1603 and became one Kingdom of Great Britain in 1707, and Ireland was added in 1801.
fact: Most of Ireland left the UK in 1922 as the Irish Free State, while six north-eastern counties stayed in the UK as Northern Ireland.
fact: About 69 million people live in the UK, and about 84 percent of them live in England.
fact: Ben Nevis in Scotland is the highest mountain in the UK at 1,345 metres, Yr Wyddfa, also called Snowdon, is the highest in Wales at 1,085 metres, and Scafell Pike is the highest in England at 978 metres; the mountains are in the north and west, where the rocks are hard and old and wear away slowly, while the south-east is softer, lower land.
fact: The River Severn is the UK's longest river at about 354 km, flowing from Wales to the Bristol Channel, and the Thames, about 346 km long, flows through London to the North Sea.
fact: Lough Neagh in Northern Ireland is the largest lake in the UK by area, while Loch Ness in Scotland, long, narrow and over 200 metres deep, holds more water than all the lakes of England and Wales together.
fact: The UK has a mild, wet climate: warm Atlantic water and westerly winds make its winters warmer than places such as Labrador at the same latitude, and the west is wetter than the east because wet Atlantic winds drop their rain on the western hills first.
fact: The Isle of Man, Jersey and Guernsey are Crown Dependencies, not part of the UK, and 14 British Overseas Territories, such as Gibraltar, Bermuda and the Falkland Islands, remain from the empire; Gibraltar, British since 1704, guards the entrance to the Mediterranean.
fact: No place in the UK is more than about 113 km, or 70 miles, from tidal water.
fact: In 1337, the year EU5 starts, England and Scotland were separate rival kingdoms and the Hundred Years' War between England and France was beginning.
exercise: What does the name British Isles cover? | Great Britain, Ireland and over 6,000 smaller islands | Only England, Scotland and Wales | Only the United Kingdom | Great Britain and France
exercise: Why does the Irish government not use the name British Isles? | Many in Ireland dislike it, as Ireland is not British | It is too long to write | The islands are not really in the Atlantic Ocean at all | It was banned by the EU
exercise: Which three nations are on the island of Great Britain? | England, Scotland and Wales | England, Wales and Northern Ireland | England, Scotland and Ireland | England, Scotland and Cornwall
exercise: Is Belfast in Great Britain, in the United Kingdom, or in both? | In the UK, but not on Great Britain | In both | In Great Britain, but not in the UK | In neither
exercise: What is the capital of Wales? | Cardiff | Swansea | Edinburgh | Belfast
exercise: What is the capital of Scotland? | Edinburgh | Glasgow | Aberdeen | Inverness
exercise: In which year did Great Britain become one kingdom? | 1707 | 1603 | 1801 | 1066
exercise: When did England and Scotland start sharing a king? | 1603 | 1707 | 1337 | 1922
exercise: When did most of Ireland leave the UK? | 1922 | 1801 | 1707 | 1998
exercise: Which nation of the UK has the most people? | England | Scotland | Wales | Northern Ireland
exercise: About how many people live in the UK? | About 69 million | About 6.9 million | About 690 million | About 340 million
exercise: Which is the highest mountain in the UK? | Ben Nevis | Scafell Pike | Yr Wyddfa, or Snowdon | Ben Lomond
exercise: Which river is longer, the Severn or the Thames? | The Severn, about 354 km | The Thames, about 346 km | They are exactly equal | The Thames, about 500 km
exercise: Which lake holds more water than all the lakes of England and Wales together? | Loch Ness | Lough Neagh | Windermere | Loch Lomond
exercise: Which is the largest lake in the UK by area? | Lough Neagh | Loch Ness | Windermere | Loch Lomond in Scotland
exercise: Is Jersey part of the United Kingdom? | No, it is a Crown Dependency | Yes, it is a county of England | Yes, it is part of Scotland | No, it belongs to France now
exercise: Which of these is a British Overseas Territory? | Gibraltar | The Isle of Man | Jersey | Guernsey
exercise: How far is the furthest place in the UK from tidal water? | About 113 km, or 70 miles | About 1,000 km, or over 600 miles | About 11 km | About 500 km
test: In EU5 you start as England in 1337. Who rules Scotland? | Its own king, as a separate rival kingdom | The English king, from London | The French king, through his allies | Nobody, it is empty
test: Why might some Irish people prefer a name such as "Britain and Ireland"? | It does not suggest Ireland belongs to Britain | It is shorter | It is the name used in the Bible for the islands | It includes France too
test: Why does the west of Britain get more rain than the east? | Wet winds from the Atlantic drop rain on the western hills first | The east is closer to the Sun | The west has more rivers that make rain | Rain clouds come from Russia
test: Why are Britain's mountains mostly in the north and west? | The hard old rocks there resisted wear, while the south-east is softer, lower land | Snow built them up over the winters | Kings piled up earth in the north | The sea pushed the land up in the west, while the east sank under the weight of its cities
test: london's in england and england's the uk innit, same thing? | No, England is one of four nations in the UK | Yes, the UK is another name for England | Yes, but only since 1922 | No, London is in Great Britain only
test: Why does London have mild winters, while Labrador at the same latitude freezes? | Warm Atlantic water and westerly winds warm Britain | London gets more hours of winter sun than anywhere else | Britain has more volcanoes | Labrador is higher up in the mountains
test: How can Loch Ness hold so much water when it is so narrow? | It is very long and very deep | Its water is heavier than sea water | It is fed by the sea through a tunnel | It is much wider underground than on top
test: Why was Ireland divided in 1921 and 1922? | Most wanted to leave the UK, but many in the north-east wanted to stay | The British wanted only the south | The island was split by an earthquake | The Pope ordered it
test: Gibraltar became British in 1704. Why is it so useful to a sea power? | It guards the entrance to the Mediterranean | It has the richest farms in Europe | It is the highest rock in Europe | It is in the middle of the Atlantic
test: Why might a country close to the sea everywhere become a sea power? | Every region has ports and sailors nearby | Its land is too poor for anything else to grow | Sea power is always cheaper than any army | Islands cannot have armies
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the relationship between Great Britain and the United Kingdom? | Great Britain is an island; the United Kingdom additionally comprises Northern Ireland | They are identical in extent | Great Britain additionally comprises Northern Ireland | The United Kingdom excludes Wales
exam: Ascertain the year in which the Kingdom of Great Britain was constituted. | Seventeen hundred and seven | Sixteen hundred and three | Eighteen hundred and one | Nineteen hundred and twenty-two
exam: Identify the status of the Bailiwick of Jersey in respect of the United Kingdom. | A Crown Dependency, not forming part of the United Kingdom | A county of England | A British Overseas Territory in the Atlantic | A region of Scotland
exam: Identify the river of the greatest length within the United Kingdom. | The River Severn | The River Thames | The River Trent | The River Tay
